{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790535070,"nonce":"s1ZGl44QMnLzWnGQzKqA7Z5monQ=","title":"Wren imports: one module per file, whichever spelling reaches it","message":"Wren's rule (WrenLift's CLI and AOT walker): `./x` and `../x` resolve against the importer's directory and are canonicalized; a bare `x` is a sibling file first. One file is one module.\n\nCaribou's seam broke that in both paths. Probe: `game/b.wren` imported from `game/*.wren` as `game/b`, `b`, `./b`, `../game/b` and `game:b`, each bumping a static counter in `B`.\n\nHosted (`caribou run`): `./b` loaded as `game/./b` and `../game/b` as `game/../game/b`, so `b` loaded three times and the counters restarted (root 1, bare 2, dot 1, up 1, ns 3). `project::relative` joined the spelling to the importer's directory without normalizing it.\n\nCompiled (`caribou build`, wasm): only the exact root-relative spelling bound, and only when the imported module sorted before its importer. `b`, `./b`, `../game/b` and `game:b` became runtime imports that found nothing, so the imported class read as a number (\"Num does not implement 'bump()'\"). `build_wren` overwrote each module's request name with its root-relative name, dropping the spelling its importers used, and fed modules to WrenLift in path order, while WrenLift's binding pass matches an import by name against earlier modules only. This is what broke examples/browser-interop (`import \"palette\"` from `game/flock`).\n\nFix: `caribou_wren::project::resolve` and `in_namespace` hold the rule; the hosted resolver uses them, and `build_wren` names every module under the roots by its root-relative name, rewrites each import to the module it names, and orders modules dependency-first. After: hosted and wasm both print \"b loaded\" once and count 1..5.","files":null}]}